1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:26,240
New England, the winter of 1960.

2
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:34,679
At this time, power from a new source went out over the lines that serve the northeastern section of the United States.

3
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:45,260
Power from the atom, generated by the Yankee Atomic Electric Company at Rowe, Massachusetts.

4
00:00:45,259 --> 00:00:56,579
This site was selected because of its convenience to existing power transmission facilities

5
00:00:56,579 --> 00:01:02,599
and abundant water source.

6
00:01:02,599 --> 00:01:14,000
Yankee Steel Containment Sphere houses the reactor and associated equipment.

7
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:20,819
In February 1960, the reactor vessel was installed.

8
00:01:20,819 --> 00:01:27,140
Fission heat from its core raises the 2000 PSI closed cycle water to an average temperature

9
00:01:27,140 --> 00:01:29,700
of 514 degrees Fahrenheit.

10
00:01:44,280 --> 00:01:47,740
It then passes through four vertical steam generators

11
00:01:47,740 --> 00:01:51,920
to produce steam at about 467 degrees Fahrenheit

12
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:56,799
and 500 psi for operation of the turbine generator.

13
00:01:57,140 --> 00:02:10,759
Among Yankee's other novel features is the addition of boron to the coolant water

14
00:02:10,759 --> 00:02:13,580
to reduce reactivity during shutdown.

15
00:02:18,920 --> 00:02:25,159
And an in-core instrumentation system for monitoring reactor core performance.

16
00:02:25,879 --> 00:02:29,000
It also provides automatic readout in the control room.

17
00:02:35,099 --> 00:02:40,079
This system indicates start-up rate and flux level for all power ranges

18
00:02:40,079 --> 00:02:44,439
and provides shutdown signals should core power become too high.

19
00:02:50,219 --> 00:02:53,819
Total control rod drop time is less than two seconds.

20
00:02:55,159 --> 00:03:07,400
In normal operating ranges, the primary plant is controlled by the semi-automatic positioning

21
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:15,400
of control rods.

22
00:03:15,400 --> 00:03:21,319
The vent and drain system facilitates draining of the primary plant for maintenance purposes

23
00:03:21,319 --> 00:03:26,319
It also permits venting and purging gases from the plant systems.

24
00:03:32,319 --> 00:03:36,319
Fluids and gases which must be discharged from the primary plant

25
00:03:36,319 --> 00:03:40,319
are first processed through the waste disposal system.

26
00:03:40,319 --> 00:03:52,500
Here it receives, contains, treats, and safely disposes of all radioactive wastes,

27
00:03:52,919 --> 00:04:00,799
so as to yield concentrated liquid wastes, solid waste, and purified water of very low activity.

28
00:04:08,859 --> 00:04:16,279
Facilities are provided for remote underwater removal and transfer of spent fuel assemblies

29
00:04:16,279 --> 00:04:19,980
from the reactor vessel to the spent fuel storage pit.

30
00:04:21,120 --> 00:04:24,939
Installation of new fuel assemblies may also be handled in this way.

31
00:04:29,079 --> 00:04:35,779
The first core was installed in July 1960 and went critical one month later.

32
00:04:35,780 --> 00:04:40,960
After various tests, the plant began generating electricity in November.

33
00:04:46,620 --> 00:04:59,100
This core contained a uniform loading of 76 fuel assemblies enriched to 3.4 weight percent of U-235 in solid cylindrical uranium dioxide pellets.

34
00:05:00,020 --> 00:05:08,020
These fuel assemblies yielded an average of 8,470 megawatt days per metric ton of contained uranium.

35
00:05:10,520 --> 00:05:17,800
The lifetime of Core One was 18 months, from mid-November 1960 to mid-May 1962.

36
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:22,960
During this time, there was an interesting example of operational flexibility.

37
00:05:22,959 --> 00:05:29,459
The plant had originally been rated at 134 megawatts electric.

38
00:05:30,459 --> 00:05:40,120
Initial power level was 125 megawatts electric, but was increased to 150 megawatts electric in June of 1961.

39
00:05:40,779 --> 00:05:46,120
On January 18, 1962, it had delivered over 1 billion kilowatt hours.

40
00:05:47,379 --> 00:05:50,859
However, power demands were at a peak at that time.

41
00:05:50,860 --> 00:06:05,580
And so, instead of refueling, advantage was taken of the reactivity made available by gradual reductions in reactor power level and temperature, thus obtaining a four-month plant operational stretch-out.

42
00:06:06,420 --> 00:06:13,560
When the reactor was finally shut down, it was still producing power at 85 megawatts electric.

43
00:06:14,080 --> 00:06:20,600
Total energy produced by the first core was over one and one-quarter billion kilowatt hours.

44
00:06:20,860 --> 00:06:33,020
The second core was identical to the first,

45
00:06:33,540 --> 00:06:39,259
except the two central fuel assemblies from core one were retained to receive extended burn-up.

46
00:06:39,780 --> 00:06:45,160
The two extended burn-up assemblies, as well as selected assemblies from core one,

47
00:06:45,680 --> 00:06:49,259
were examined in detail and found to be in excellent condition.

48
00:06:50,860 --> 00:06:58,980
After refueling, the plant was returned to operation on September 20th, 1962.

49
00:07:00,460 --> 00:07:07,580
Early in October, power level was increased from 150 to 170 megawatts electric.

50
00:07:08,480 --> 00:07:12,560
Lifetime of the second core was one year less 18 days.

51
00:07:12,560 --> 00:07:18,980
And total energy output was just under one and one quarter billion kilowatt hours.

52
00:07:20,860 --> 00:07:35,699
The successful operation of Core 1 assemblies in Core 2 to 22,248 megawatt days per metric ton

53
00:07:35,759 --> 00:07:42,319
produced data which confirmed the ability of Yankee fuel assemblies to withstand long exposure.

54
00:07:43,540 --> 00:07:48,199
Longer exposure enables higher burnout, with resulting lower fuel costs.

55
00:07:48,199 --> 00:08:00,480
Core 3 was a multi-region core containing both partially spent core 2 assemblies and fresh fuel enriched to 4.1 weight percent of U-235.

56
00:08:01,920 --> 00:08:13,300
Core 3 was installed during October 1963, and after routine tests, the plant resumed generating electricity on November 12, 1963.

57
00:08:13,300 --> 00:08:20,000
Plant output was raised to 185 megawatts electric at the close of the year.

58
00:08:21,960 --> 00:08:27,379
Its two-region cycle loading pattern is expected to achieve an average discharge fuel burn-up

59
00:08:27,379 --> 00:08:32,340
much greater than could have been obtained from two single-region cores.

60
00:08:43,299 --> 00:08:51,559
Today Yankee is operated by a total staff of 69. Maintenance records show that approximately

61
00:08:51,559 --> 00:08:59,620
160 man hours per month are expended on primary plant instrumentation and control. About 80 hours

62
00:08:59,620 --> 00:09:06,419
per month on secondary and auxiliary plant components and about 280 man hours per month

63
00:09:06,419 --> 00:09:14,659
on-site service facilities, radiation monitoring, radiochemistry, equipment maintenance, and miscellaneous work.

64
00:09:18,639 --> 00:09:26,740
For the month of January 1964, plant utilization was 98.28%.

65
00:09:26,740 --> 00:09:32,059
This demonstrates a typical month when no refueling activities were in progress.

66
00:09:36,419 --> 00:09:41,639
Another measure of its efficiency has been cost.

67
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:47,919
The average cost per kilowatt hour of power delivered has been just over 10 mils

68
00:09:47,919 --> 00:09:53,639
as compared to the cost of conventional power in the area, which is 8 to 10 mils.

69
00:09:56,779 --> 00:10:02,059
Yankee was designed as a production plant for a group of privately owned utility companies.

70
00:10:02,719 --> 00:10:05,159
They've had almost four years experience with it,

71
00:10:05,159 --> 00:10:15,539
And the best proof that they're satisfied with its performance lies in the fact that they have already ordered another similar plant for operation in 1967.

72
00:10:19,319 --> 00:10:31,699
The Yankee plant design, based on simplicity, reliability, and safety, has proved itself and made a significant contribution to the new art of nuclear power.
